A||||4||Cooter||Raining inside my house?||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||10-20-2000||07:12 PM||||Wednesday afternoon, I came home to find an upstairs water pipe had broken during the day and flooded 3 upstairs bedrooms and my entire downstairs living room/office/computer work center. All my previous emails with forum members that I printed are gone- (Rockcrawl email me about the tranny if you still want it) as are lots of my school work books, Nascar collectible items, a couple of computer moniters and more than I can think to list. Both my wife's and my computers barely escaped the flood, but I pulled them apart to ensure that no water condensed inside. It has been a real pain in the butt, so for my pain I will offer this advice: Check your toilet's fill line. If it has one with a plastic nut that holds it to the toilet- Replace the entire line with one with a metal nut! The plastic nut on mine broke in half and let the o-ring slip down and cause the leak. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||hugh||10-20-2000||08:27 PM||||Your homeowners insurance should cover the damage,but not the pipe repair.||||reg||14
Z||000002||Raydar||10-20-2000||11:49 PM||||Cooter, <P>I'll bet that made you say "darn!"<P>Sorry about that. Not attempting to make light of the situation. I'd be really chapped.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||10-21-2000||04:39 PM||||Well, two people have been out to look at the house. One wants to just dry the carpet and patch up the rest of the house. The other wants to come in and strip everything and replace all carpet, paneling and sheetrock. Since this has happened, my allergies have gone crazy, so my intentions are to go with the contractor that wants to strip out everything. I guess the adjuster will have his say so before I can go on. It will be nice to sleep in my own bed again, maybe everything will dry soon.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-21-2000||06:58 PM||||I stopped using the traditional copper or plastic semi ridged lines on all my sinks and toylets. I use the prefab hose sets now. They only need to be finger tight or a very slight wrenching to get a perfect seal. No more split/stripped slip nuts. No more trying to bend lines w/o kinking. You can hook a sink up in 5 minutes with these. Plus being flexible they won't easily get wrecked by line hammering. Well worth the 3-5$ each for them.||||reg||1
